> True in english, but not in lojban. In lojban, 100% of the men
> is either
> lu'a piro le'i nanmu

= ro lu'a piro le'i nanmu, presumably

> or
> piro lei nanmu
> Which are both "one of the [masses/sets of] men". Or the "whole of" it,
> to phrase it in an english friendly way 
> 
> In lojban you can't say
> piro le nanmu
> unless you mean
> pa le nanmu
> and even then you're probably talking weirdly 
> 
> So,
> ro le nanmu
> is all of the men, and not the same as {piro} of them 

What I meant is that

"100% of men"
= ro lu'a pi ro lo'i nanmu
= ro nanmu

"50% of men"
= ro lu'a pi mu lo'i nanmu

"most men"
= ro lu'a pi so'e lo'i nanmu
= so'e nanmu

